matt: FYI, sample-app deployment to Cloudflare Workers is failing. stack shows ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND: fs when importing @redwood/inference.
caro: ouch. which bundle did you use? browser or node?
matt: pulled from the app's deps — default import points to the CJS/node entry in dist. Worker runtime has no fs so it crashes at require('fs') in our node-utils. :facepalm: 
frank: Workers have global fetch, Headers, Request, Response — but no Node APIs. We should ensure we ship a worker/browser-safe build. Could be package.json exports are wrong.
sara: types also blow up in the sample-app CI: TS expects NodeJS.ReadableStream in a couple of places. Workers use DOM streams. Need to fix public types.
caro: short-term workaround for teams: explicitly construct a worker-friendly client in the app and pass in the platform fetch + a minimal auth hook. e.g.
```js
// worker-friendly init (in sample-app)
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference/dist/browser.mjs';
const client = new RedwoodClient({
  baseURL: BUILTIN_API,
  fetch: globalThis.fetch.bind(globalThis),
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${TOKEN}` }
});
```
matt: that gets past the fs import but we still hit code that uses crypto.randomBytes for nonce. Workers expose crypto.getRandomValues. I patched a tiny util in the app for now: 
```js
function randHex(n) {
  const arr = new Uint8Array(n);
  (globalThis.crypto || {}).getRandomValues(arr);
  return Array.from(arr).map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');
}
```
frank: long-term we should: 1) split entrypoints (browser/worker vs node), 2) remove Node-only shims from the runtime bundle, 3) adjust package.json exports to point to an ESM worker-safe entry. Example exports: 
````json
{"./package.json": {"default": "./package.json"},".": {"browser": "./dist/index.browser.mjs","node": "./dist/index.node.cjs"}}
````
(we'll refine exact format)
sara: for typings: stop exposing Node stream types in the public API. Options: use DOM types only, or provide separate node typings at a node-only entrypoint. Also avoid `Buffer`/`fs` types leaking.
caro: small conditional in runtime to pick random bytes is fine but use ESM dynamic imports for node parts to avoid bundlers pulling node-only code into browser builds: 
```js
const isNode = typeof process !== 'undefined' && !!process.versions?.node;
export const randomBytes = isNode ? async (n)=> (await import('crypto')).randomBytes(n) : (n)=>{ const a=new Uint8Array(n); crypto.getRandomValues(a); return a; };
```
matt: another gotcha: auth code builds a `Request` with `new Headers()` then calls `req.clone()` and streams it into a Node-only helper that expects Node streams. We should avoid cloning into Node stream APIs in the shared path. Keep shared code DOM-first.
frank: propose we publish v0.9.3 patch that: - exposes dist/index.browser.mjs as default for platforms with globalThis.fetch - moves node-only helpers behind dist/node entry - removes direct fs/Buffer uses from runtime core - adds a small runtime check to use getRandomValues if crypto.randomBytes isn't present
caro: action items: I'll open an issue and a sample-app PR showing the minimal worker-compatible usage. frank, can you draft the patch? sara, can you audit exported types for Node collisions?
frank: yep, I'll prepare the patch and add a tiny CI job that bundles+executes a worker preview via wrangler to catch regressions. target: v0.9.3 by tomorrow. :rocket:
sara: I'll grep the repo for NodeJS.* and Buffer/ReadableStream references and drop a list in the issue.
devx-bot: deploy-bot: sample-app worker preview build succeeded — https://preview.workers.example/abc123 :)
matt: great, thanks folks. once patch lands I can revert the temporary worker hacks in the app. :tada:
caro: also note: update docs/onboarding guide with a short section "Using Redwood in edge runtimes" with a sample snippet and caveats about types and node-only features.
frank: will add tests and the doc change to the PR.
caro: closing loop: no blockers, we'll track progress in the issue. thanks! :thumbsup:
